Keep a smart component without pole layers as a regular component

Upstream, merged 2026-09-28 (after the v6.15.0 release):
  PR:            https://github.com/googlefonts/glyphsLib/pull/1177
  merge commit:  088deb52a0a1af2eefe40e0d302f78f9f15266ca
  commits:       36c208a35 (fallback), d3510e790 (anthrotype: only when no
                 layer of the glyph has a pole mapping; log once per glyph
                 at DEBUG; matches googlefonts/fontc#2186)
  Issue:         https://github.com/googlefonts/glyphsLib/issues/1176

This is the combined upstream diff for the two library files,
`git diff v6.15.0...088deb52 -- Lib/glyphsLib/builder/`, unchanged.
Drop this patch, and the matching package test, when the glyphslib pinned
by uv (see deps.txt in the build log) is a release that contains the merge
commit; --forward in the patch step fails loudly when that happens.

Why: since glyphsLib 6.13.0 (googlefonts/glyphsLib#1135) a glyph that
declares smart-component axes but has no layer mapped to a pole made the
whole build fail with "Could not find any masters for the smart
component". Noto Sans Bengali has 22 such glyphs. Glyphs.app exports them
as regular components; so does this. If only some masters lack poles the
build still raises, as upstream does, because a component in one master
and an outline in another cannot interpolate.

Applied to the installed wheel (the wheel drops the sdist's Lib/ prefix,
hence -p2).

diff --git a/Lib/glyphsLib/builder/builders.py b/Lib/glyphsLib/builder/builders.py
--- a/Lib/glyphsLib/builder/builders.py
+++ b/Lib/glyphsLib/builder/builders.py
@@ -103,6 +103,7 @@ def __init__(
         self.bracket_layers = []
         self.write_skipexportglyphs = write_skipexportglyphs
         self.skip_export_glyphs = set()
+        self._smart_glyphs_without_poles = set()
         self.expand_includes = expand_includes
         self.minimal = minimal
 
diff --git a/Lib/glyphsLib/builder/smart_components.py b/Lib/glyphsLib/builder/smart_components.py
--- a/Lib/glyphsLib/builder/smart_components.py
+++ b/Lib/glyphsLib/builder/smart_components.py
@@ -20,13 +20,16 @@
 OpenType interpolation model to adjust the node positions.
 """
 
+import logging
 from enum import IntEnum
 
 from fontTools.varLib.models import VariationModel, normalizeValue, VariationModelError
 from fontTools.ttLib.tables._g_l_y_f import GlyphCoordinates
 
 from glyphsLib.classes import GSLayer
 
+logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)
+
 
 # smartComponentPoleMapping returns 1 for bottom of axis and 2 for top.
 class Pole(IntEnum):
@@ -135,6 +138,10 @@ def get_smart_component_variation_model(layer, component):
     root = component.component
 
     masters = [l for l in root.layers if l.smartComponentPoleMapping]
+    if not masters:
+        # No layer of the glyph is mapped to a pole: nothing to interpolate,
+        # use it as a regular component (see instantiate_smart_component).
+        return None, None, None
     if layer.associatedMasterId:
         # Each master in the font can have its own set of smart component
         # "master layers", so we need to filter by those smart components
@@ -176,6 +183,22 @@ def instantiate_smart_component(self, layer, component, pen):
     # Find the GSGlyph that is being used as a component by this GSComponent
     root = component.component
 
+    # A glyph can declare smart component axes without any layer mapped to a
+    # pole (e.g. left over from duplicating a smart glyph). There is nothing to
+    # interpolate, and Glyphs.app exports it as a regular component.
+    # If only some masters lack poles we still raise below, since keeping a
+    # component in one master and decomposing it in another is incompatible.
+    if not any(l.smartComponentPoleMapping for l in root.layers):
+        if root.name not in self._smart_glyphs_without_poles:
+            self._smart_glyphs_without_poles.add(root.name)
+            logger.debug(
+                "Smart component %s has no layers mapped to poles; "
+                "keeping it as a regular component",
+                root.name,
+            )
+        pen.addComponent(component.name, component.transform)
+        return
+
     model, normalized_location, masters = get_smart_component_variation_model(
         layer, component
     )
